## Preliminaries: check your setup

Evaluate this cell after setup finishes. A **satisfiable** result showing `ready`
confirms that the bundled clingo is running inside the notebook. Each solve
closes its own native control; this check is safe to reevaluate.

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("../support/course.exs", __DIR__))
alias ASPCourse, as: Course
alias Clingo.Control

Clingo.solve!("ready. #show ready/0.") |> Course.display()
```

## Inspect atoms and exchange grounded programs

### What you will learn

Distinguish copied inspection, ASPIF export, reification and DIMACS input.

### Copy atom information before closing

Inspection exposes grounded symbolic atoms with solver literals and fact/external flags. The returned data remain readable after closure. Literal numbers identify this grounding and must not be treated as persistent IDs across controls.

```elixir
atoms =
  Clingo.with_control(fn control ->
    Clingo.Control.add!(control, "fixed. {optional}. #external input.")
    Clingo.Control.ground!(control)
    Clingo.Inspection.symbolic_atoms!(control)
  end)

by_name = Map.new(atoms, &{Clingo.Symbol.format!(&1.symbol), &1})
true = by_name["fixed"].fact
true = by_name["input"].external
true = is_integer(by_name["optional"].literal)
Enum.sort(Map.keys(by_name))
```

### Export a program without searching it

ASPIF is a grounded program format. Reified output describes that program as ordinary facts for a meta-interpreter. Solving reified facts alone does not reconstruct the original answers. This example checks an ASPIF round trip; it only inspects the reification.

```elixir
program = "{permit}. admitted :- permit. #show admitted/0."
aspif = Clingo.Ground.to_string!(program)
reified = Clingo.Ground.to_string!(program, format: :reify)
true = String.starts_with?(aspif, "asp 1 0 0")
true = String.contains?(reified, "rule(")
original = Clingo.solve!(program, models: :all)
round_trip = Clingo.solve!(aspif, models: :all)

true =
  Enum.sort(Enum.map(original.models, & &1.shown)) ==
    Enum.sort(Enum.map(round_trip.models, & &1.shown))

IO.puts(reified)
```

### Read a tiny CNF instance natively

The DIMACS clauses require variable 1 and allow either truth value for variable 2. The adapter presents true variables as v(N), giving two models. No command-line solver is involved.

```elixir
result =
  Clingo.Dimacs.solve!("p cnf 2 2
1 0
1 2 0
",
    models: :all
  )

[["v(1)"], ["v(1)", "v(2)"]] = Enum.sort(Enum.map(result.models, & &1.shown))
Course.display(result)
```

### Try it yourself

Change the first DIMACS clause to -1 0. Work out the second clause before running.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

The first clause forces variable 1 false. The clause 1 2 then forces variable 2 true, so the only shown answer is v(2).

</details>

## Inspect ASPIF across incremental steps

```elixir
workdir =
  Course.workspace()
```

Ground export exposes the program passed to the solver. In an incremental workflow, each added part contributes a new step rather than requiring a fresh process. Inspect the available ASPIF examples and distinguish atom definitions, output directives and step boundaries.

Native `Clingo.Ground.to_string!` handles ordinary exports; use scoped controls for solving related program parts. Numeric identifiers are implementation details and should not become expected answer labels.

```elixir
Course.write(workdir, "example.lp", ~S"""
dom(1..2).
a(Item) :- dom(Item).
b(Item) :- not a(Item), dom(Item).
{ c(Item) } :- dom(Item).
d(Item) :- not c(Item), dom(Item).
e(Item); f(Item); g(Item) :- c(Item), d(Item), dom(Item).
{ e(Item); f(Item); g(Item) } :- c(Item), d(Item), dom(Item).
h :- 1 { e(Item); f(Item) }.
i :- 1 { e(Item); f(Item) } 1.
:- h, i.
#show (e(Item),f(Item),g(Item)) : e(Item), f(Item), g(Item).
#minimize{ 1@3,Item : e(Item); 2@3,Item : f(Item); 3,Item : g(Item)}.
#external j. [true]
#heuristic e(Item) : c(Item). [1,level]
#project e/1.
""")
```

```elixir
Clingo.Ground.to_string!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "example.lp")])
|> IO.puts()
```

### Workspace cleanup

Save edited files before running cleanup. Rerun this section’s workspace cell to start again.

```elixir
File.rm_rf!(workdir)
:ok
```
